  "response": "**VERDICT: REVISE**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component uses a native `<input type=\"file\">` (correct), but the accessibility design decisions around labeling and state communication are incomplete. The label is decoupled from the input via a plain `<div>`, and validation state is rendered visually without programmatic binding to assistive technology. These are architectural gaps that automated scanners may flag as violations, but the deeper design failure is the missing `aria-invalid`/`aria-describedby` contract and semantic association pattern.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**: Before reading code, I predicted:\n1. Label decoupled from input (plain `<div>` instead of `<label>`)\n2. Error state rendered visually only, missing `aria-invalid` and `aria-describedby`\n3. No visible help text for file type restrictions despite `accept` attribute\n4. Missing `id`/`htmlFor` pairing preventing programmatic association\n5. Potential color-only error indication (common pattern)\n\nAll predictions materialized during investigation.\n\n**Critical Findings** (blocks access):\n1. **Label decoupled from input via non-semantic container**\n   - Evidence: `src/BuggyFileInput.jsx:18` (`<div>Upload file</div>`) and `src/BuggyFileInput.jsx:20` (`<input type=\"file\" ... />`) lack `id`/`htmlFor` pairing or `aria-labelledby`.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users, keyboard-only users relying on AT.\n   - WCAG/APG citation: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value). WAI-ARIA Forms pattern requires explicit label association.\n   - Fix: Replace the wrapper `<div>` with `<label htmlFor=\"file-input\">Upload file</label>` and add `id=\"file-input\"` to the `<input>`.\n\n2. **Validation state not communicated to assistive technology**\n   - Evidence: `src/BuggyFileInput.jsx:26-30` renders error visually but never sets `aria-invalid` on the input or links the message via `aria-describedby`.\n   - User group impacted: Screen reader users (error state is silent), cognitive accessibility users (unclear which field failed).\n   - WCAG/APG citation: WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages), WCAG 3.3.1 (Error Identification). APG Form Validation pattern requires `aria-invalid=\"true\"` on the control and `aria-describedby` pointing to the error message.\n   - Fix: Add `aria-invalid={!!error}` to the input, add `id=\"file-error\"` to the error `<div>`, and set `aria-describedby={error ? 'file-error' : undefined}` on the input.\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **File type restrictions not visible or programmatically associated**\n   - Evidence: `src/BuggyFileInput.jsx:23` uses `accept=\".pdf,.doc,.docx\"` but no visible text explains this constraint. Screen readers do not consistently announce `accept` values across UAs.\n   - User group impacted: All users, especially cognitive and screen reader users who need explicit instructions.\n   - WCAG/APG citation: WCAG 3.3.2 (Labels or Instructions), WCAG 1.3.1.\n   - Fix: Add visible help text (e.g., `<span id=\"file-help\">Accepted formats: PDF, DOC, DOCX</span>`) and associate it via `aria-describedby` on the input alongside the error ID.\n\n2. **Error message lacks non-color indicator**\n   - Evidence: Visual-only styling implied by `.error-message` class. No icon, border, or text prefix in markup.\n   - User group impacted: Low vision users, colorblind users.\n   - WCAG/APG citation: WCAG 1.4.1 (Use of Color).\n   - Fix: Add a non-color indicator (e.g., `\u26a0` icon with `aria-hidden=\"true\"`, or prefix text like \"Error: \") to the error message.\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- **Selection state not announced**: `selectedFile` state exists but isn't communicated to AT. If the UI later shows the selected filename, it needs `aria-live=\"polite\"` or a status region. Currently silent.\n- **No visible file name preview**: Users relying on screen readers won't know which file was selected unless explicitly announced. Consider adding `<span id=\"file-name\" aria-live=\"polite\">{selectedFile?.name}</span>` if UI supports it.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- Wrap the component in a `<fieldset>` with a `<legend>` if part of a larger form, to group related controls semantically.\n- Add `aria-describedby` pointing to help text even when no error exists, so the association persists: `aria-describedby=\"file-help file-error\"` (with conditional rendering for `file-error`).\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- Missing programmatic state contract: The component manages `error` in React state but treats it as a visual-only concern. No `aria-invalid`, no `aria-describedby`, no `role=\"alert\"` or `aria-live` for dynamic updates.\n- Missing visible instructions: `accept` attribute is a browser filter, not a user-facing instruction. WCAG requires explicit text instructions for file type constraints.\n- Missing focus management on validation failure: If the form submits and fails, focus isn't programmatically moved to the error message or input (though this component lacks a submit button, the pattern should be noted for integration).\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | Label decoupled, `aria-invalid` missing, error silent |\n| Keyboard-only | MEDIUM | Tab order logical, but validation feedback invisible to AT |\n| Low vision | MEDIUM | Error likely color-only; text contrast unverified in code |\n| Cognitive | HIGH | Unclear field association; error message not linked to input |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations/motion present |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No media elements |\n| Environmental contrast | MEDIUM | Error styling likely relies on color without fallback |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component fails the core accessibility design contract for form controls: explicit labeling and programmatic state communication. While axe-core would flag the missing label as a violation, the deeper design gap is the architectural decision to separate label from input and omit `aria-invalid`/`aria-describedby`. This breaks the interaction model for assistive technology users who rely on these attributes to understand field purpose and validation state. The verdict is REVISE because the labeling and error association patterns are incomplete per WAI-ARIA APG Forms and Validation patterns. Upgrading to ACCEPT requires: (1) semantic `<label>` with `htmlFor`/`id`, (2) `aria-invalid` synced to error state, (3) `aria-describedby` linking input to error/help text, and (4) non-color error indicator.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the parent form handle submission? If so, does it move focus to the error message on validation failure? (Requires integration context)\n- Is `.error-message` styled with color-only indicators? (Requires CSS audit)\n- Will `selectedFile` be displayed in the UI? If yes, how is it announced to AT? (Requires future-state design review)\n\n*Self-Audit & Realist Check*: Confidence in label and error association findings is HIGH. These are foundational WCAG 1.3.1/4.1.2 requirements with direct user impact. Severity calibrated: MAJOR for missing state communication (degrades but doesn't block access), CRITICAL for missing label association (blocks programmatic understanding). No manufactured violations; all findings trace to explicit code gaps.",
